From 0e309b5cbabe2d9a6075bee70a2f7c59f5cc298a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:50:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/17] feat(control-plane): add controller, allocation and
 workflow metrics

Operators need to see whether the controller keeps up and what the
cluster looks like, so /metrics now also covers:

- forgemesh_reconcile_total{operation,outcome} and
  forgemesh_reconcile_duration_seconds{operation}, recorded by the
  controller manager around each reconcile (outcomes done, requeue,
  wait, conflict, error);
- forgemesh_controller_retries_total and
  forgemesh_controller_queue_depth{state};
- forgemesh_deployments_total{outcome,reason}: status writes that move
  a service into Ready or Failed, with the Ready condition reason;
- forgemesh_inference_services{phase},
  forgemesh_allocations_active{accelerator_type},
  forgemesh_accelerator_devices{accelerator_type,state} and
  forgemesh_workflow_runs{status}, read from the registry, the
  inventory and the workflow store at scrape time;
- forgemesh_database_lock_wait_seconds, the time database operations
  wait for the connection lock that the registry, its outbox (and so
  the event dispatcher) and the audit log share. Database.call and
  Database.transaction report the wait to an observer that /metrics
  registers on the registry's database.

The hooks stay in the controller manager (a wrapper around the
reconcile body and one line in the retry path), so the reconciler and
the deployment code are untouched; deployment outcomes are derived
from the status patch the manager writes. Labels are fixed enums,
accelerator types from the operator's inventory and sanitised
condition reasons; tenants, names and uids never become labels. The
new registry and workflow store queries aggregate across tenants and
are only used for /metrics.
